WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.707
A recommendation heuristic.

2
00:00:01.987 --> 00:00:04.013
Start from the work, not the framework.

3
00:00:04.293 --> 00:00:12.635
Week 1’s first question still applies: if a single call or a fixed workflow solves the task, you don’t need an agent framework at all.

4
00:00:12.915 --> 00:00:16.904
If you do need an agent, walk down this list and stop at the first match.

5
00:00:17.184 --> 00:00:19.979
Is it a short loop with a handful of your own tools?

6
00:00:20.259 --> 00:00:23.267
Write plain code, or use your provider’s tool runner.

7
00:00:23.547 --> 00:00:27.387
You will understand every line, and the lab shows it fits in a page.

8
00:00:27.667 --> 00:00:34.643
Does the agent mainly read and edit files, run commands, and search a codebase or documents on a machine you control?

9
00:00:34.923 --> 00:00:40.597
Use the Claude Agent SDK rather than rebuilding Claude Code’s tools and permission system.

10
00:00:40.877 --> 00:00:51.032
Should it run for minutes or hours, on a schedule, or without you operating servers and sandboxes, and is your data policy compatible with a hosted, stateful service?

11
00:00:51.312 --> 00:00:53.040
Look at Claude Managed Agents.

12
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:59.763
Must it pause for people and resume later, survive crashes, or follow an explicit multi-branch graph?

13
00:01:00.043 --> 00:01:02.965
LangGraph’s persistence and interrupts are built for that.

14
00:01:03.245 --> 00:01:08.856
Is it multi-agent with handoffs, or must it run across several providers or open models?

15
00:01:09.136 --> 00:01:19.995
The OpenAI Agents SDK or smolagents give you provider-neutral loops; pick by which primitives (handoffs and guardrails, or code actions) fit the task.

16
00:01:20.275 --> 00:01:23.667
Then apply three checks before you commit, whichever you chose.

17
00:01:23.947 --> 00:01:29.707
Defaults: write down its limits, permission mode, and where traces go, and set each one explicitly.

18
00:01:29.987 --> 00:01:34.061
Exit cost: could you move to plain code in a week if the framework stalled?

19
00:01:34.341 --> 00:01:43.173
Evidence: run your Studio 3 eval suite on a plain-code baseline and on the framework version, and compare success, cost per task, and steps.

20
00:01:43.453 --> 00:01:49.448
A framework that does not beat or match the baseline on your own tasks is a dependency, not a tool.
